NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001325 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001326 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001327 "=item * B<$a0> => I<str>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001328 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001329 "(No description)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001331 "=item * B<$a1> => I<str>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001332 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001333 "(No description)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001334 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001335 "=item * B<$a2> => I<str>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001336 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001337 "(No description)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001338 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001339 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001340 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001341 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001342 "Returns an enveloped result (an array)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001343 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001344 "First element ($status_code) is an integer containing HTTP-like status code"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001345 "(200 means OK, 4xx caller error, 5xx function error). Second element"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001346 "($reason) is a string containing error message, or something like \"OK\" if status is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001347 "200. Third element ($payload) is the actual result, but usually not present when enveloped result is an error response ($status_code is not 2xx). Fourth"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001348 "element (%result_meta) is called result metadata and is optional, a hash"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001349 "that contains extra information, much like how HTTP response headers provide additional metadata."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001350 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001351 "Return value:  (any)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001352 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001354 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001355 "=head2 args_as_hashref"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001356 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001357 "Usage:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001358 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001359 " args_as_hashref(\\%args) -> [$status_code, $reason, $payload, \\%result_meta]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001360 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001361 "This function's metadata sets C<args_as> property to C<hashref>. This is just like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001362 "C<hash>, except the whole argument hash is passed in C<$_[0]>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001363 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001364 "This function is not exported."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001365 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001366 "Arguments ('*' denotes required arguments):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001367 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001368 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001369 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001370 "=item * B<a0> => I<str>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001371 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001372 "(No description)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001374 "=item * B<a1> => I<str>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001375 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001376 "(No description)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001377 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001378 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001379 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001380 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001381 "Returns an enveloped result (an array)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001382 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001383 "First element ($status_code) is an integer containing HTTP-like status code"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001384 "(200 means OK, 4xx caller error, 5xx function error). Second element"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001385 "($reason) is a string containing error message, or something like \"OK\" if status is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001386 "200. Third element ($payload) is the actual result, but usually not present when enveloped result is an error response ($status_code is not 2xx). Fourth"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001387 "element (%result_meta) is called result metadata and is optional, a hash"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001388 "that contains extra information, much like how HTTP response headers provide additional metadata."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001389 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001390 "Return value:  (any)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001391 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001392 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001393 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001394 "=head2 binary"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001395 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001396 "Usage:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001397 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001398 " binary(%args) -> [$status_code, $reason, $payload, \\%result_meta]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001399 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001400 "Accept and send binary data."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001401 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001402 "This function sets its argument's schema type as C<buf> which indicates the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001403 "argument accepts binary data. Likewise it also sets its result's schema type as"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001404 "C<buf> which says that function will return binary data."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001405 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001406 "The function just returns its argument."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001407 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001408 "Note that since the metadata also contains null (\"\\0\") in the C<default> property"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001409 "of the argument specification, the metadata is also not JSON-safe."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001410 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001411 "To pass binary data over JSON/Riap, you can use Riap version 1.2 and encode the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001412 "argument with \":base64\" suffix, e.g.:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001413 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001414 " $res = Perinci::Access->new->request("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001415 "     call => \"http://example.com/api/Perinci/Examples/binary\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001416 "     {v=>1.2, args=>{\"data:base64\"=>\"/wA=\"}}); # send \"\\xff\\0\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001417 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001418 "Without C<< v=E<gt>1.2 >>, encoded argument won't be decoded by the server."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001419 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001420 "To pass binary data on the command-line, you can use C<--ARG-base64> if the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001421 "command-line library provides it."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001422 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001423 "To receive binary result over JSON/Riap, you can use Riap version 1.2 which will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001424 "automatically encode binary data with base64 so it is safe when transformed as"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001425 "JSON. The client library will also decode the encoded result back to the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001426 "original, so the whole process is transparent to you:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001427 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001428 " $res = Perinci::Access->new->request("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001429 "     call => \"http://example.com/api/Perinci/Examples/binary\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001430 "     {v=>1.2}); # => [200,\"OK\",\"\\0\\0\\0\",{}]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001431 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001432 "This function is not exported."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001433 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001434 "Arguments ('*' denotes required arguments):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001435 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001436 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001437 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001438 "=item * B<data> => I<buf> (default: \"\\0\\0\\0\")"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001439 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001440 "(No description)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001441 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001442 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001443 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001444 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001445 "Returns an enveloped result (an array)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001446 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001447 "First element ($status_code) is an integer containing HTTP-like status code"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001448 "(200 means OK, 4xx caller error, 5xx function error). Second element"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001449 "($reason) is a string containing error message, or something like \"OK\" if status is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001450 "200. Third element ($payload) is the actual result, but usually not present when enveloped result is an error response ($status_code is not 2xx). Fourth"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001451 "element (%result_meta) is called result metadata and is optional, a hash"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001452 "that contains extra information, much like how HTTP response headers provide additional metadata."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001453 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001454 "Return value:  (buf)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001455 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001456 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001457 ""
